  "finding_info": {
    "uid": "500023b0fe39663683bc17b526de8b11",
    "title": "Sudo GTFOBin breakout to root",
    "desc": "A program ran sudo against a GTFOBin and carried a shell-escape token in the same command - sudo find ... -exec /bin/sh, sudo awk 'BEGIN{system(...)}', sudo vim -c ':!sh', sudo emacs --eval '(term \"/bin/sh\")', sudo python -c 'pty.spawn(\"/bin/sh\")'. Allowing an account to sudo one of these utilities hands it a root shell, so the escape token next to a GTFOBin is a far higher-signal privilege escalation than sudo on its own (MITRE T1548.003 Sudo and Sudo Caching). privilege_escalation_attempt fires on any sudo; this fires only on the sudo-to-root breakout shape, so it is the one to page on.",
